  "result": "I wrote the 10-minute lesson for O3 at `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. I haven't viewed it in a browser, counted its words, or checked its links. The two links are the pages I fetched this session.\n\nIt covers:\n- **The context window:** it is resent in full on every call and grows with each step. A figure shows this.\n- **Cost and context rot:** a bigger pile costs more and works worse. It also covers compaction and tool-result clearing.\n- **Memory between sessions:** a session's context is gone when it ends, so anything the assistant must know next time has to be saved as notes outside the context. The example is a student's training status.\n- **Retrieval:** a `search_manual` tool loads only the relevant manual passage instead of the whole manual.\n- **Worked example:** one booking request for the laser cutter, in five small steps. Then a 3D printer question for you to work through, with the solution hidden until you ask for it.\n\nThe lesson also has a recall warm-up on the agent loop, one multiple-choice check, a summary and a \"say it in your own words\" prompt.\n\nI added both sources to `RESOURCES.md` and a line to `NOTES.md` saying the lesson is written but not yet quizzed. I did not write a cheat sheet or any learning record. I didn't add a community pointer.\n\nYou can ask for a shorter or longer version, ask me anything about it in the chat, or ask for a quiz (`/quiz`) when you're ready.\n\nOne question for you: the lesson assumes you'll store student training status in a file or database you load at session start. Is that how your team plans to do it, or do you want the next lesson to compare other options?",
